 \subsection{Self-evolution of the AI annotator}
 \label{app:autoannot-dev}
-We build the AI annotator based on OverReal-Det. We split the gold images in half,
-stratified by family and generator, into a development set and a test set,
-and the development half alone drives the self-evolution. 
-The annotator is adapted to the task without updating any model weights,
-by a form of self-evolution: the model iteratively annotate on the development set with a guideline and modify the guideline according the mistakes it made in the annotation, until the AI annotator a high agreement with human labels. We further verify the performance of the AI annotator on the test to verify its generalizability.
-
-The form of the
-questions was fixed first: in a pilot with a 7B vision-language model,
-multiple-choice prompts with a hedged third option reached $\kappa=0.23$ to
-$0.80$ on images that a binary question scored at $0.94$ to $1.00$, so
-every question is binary. Each round then scores the development half,
-collects the images on which the label disagrees with the human one, and
-hands them back to the model, which reads them, traces each disagreement to
-the question that produced it, and proposes a rewrite of that question; we
-accept the rewrite and score the revised set again. Seven rounds took the
-annotator from its first wording to the final one, with the wording
-selected per family. The result was frozen and the test half was scored
-once, with Claude Opus~5 \citep{anthropic2026opus5}. A second annotator was
-built from GPT-5.2 \citep{openai2025gpt52} on the same frozen questions,
-without further revision.
+
+The AI annotator is adapted to the task without updating any model weights,
+by a form of self-evolution: the model annotates a development set with its
+current questions, revises the questions from the mistakes it made, and
+repeats until its agreement with the human labels stops improving. We build
+it on the human labels of OverReal-Det, split in half, stratified by family
+and generator, into a development set and a test set; the development half
+alone drives the self-evolution, and the frozen annotator is then evaluated
+once on the test set. In each round the model scores the development half,
+receives the images on which its label disagrees with the human one, traces
+each disagreement to the question that produced it, and proposes a rewrite
+of that question; we accept the rewrite and score the revised questions
+again. The best wording is selected per family.
 
 \subsection{Agreement with the human labels}
 \label{app:autoannot-results}
 
-Table~\ref{tab:autoannot} reports the annotator against the human gold
-labels on the test half. Conclusions are given as follows.
+The annotator runs on Claude Opus~5 \citep{anthropic2026opus5}; a second
+annotator built from GPT-5.2 \citep{openai2025gpt52} uses the same frozen
+questions without further revision. Table~\ref{tab:autoannot} reports the
+Opus annotator against the human gold labels on the test half. Conclusions
+are given as follows.
+
+\paragraph{The evolved questions agree with the human labels far better
+than a direct label request, and better than their first version where
+that version was weak.} Asking for a label outright is unreliable: in a
+pilot with a 7B vision-language model, a multiple-choice prompt with a
+hedged third option reached $\kappa=0.23$ to $0.80$ on images that a single
+binary question scored at $0.94$ to $1.00$, which is why every question of
+the cascade is binary. Seven rounds of self-evolution then raised the
+agreement on the development half where the first version was weakest,
+from $\kappa=0.39$ to $0.59$ on perspectival prompts and from $0.56$ to
+$0.59$ on mental-state prompts, while existence-canceling ($0.88$) and
+figurative ($0.66$) prompts kept their first wording; the rewrites of later
+rounds did not improve on it, and tuning stopped.
 
